# Training Large Models at One-Tenth the Cost, With Better Results: How Is That Possible?

In 2025, the reinforcement learning compute bill for training a model comparable to GPT-4 was roughly several million dollars. Over the past six months, leading models such as Qwen3, Gemma 2, and MiMo have all shifted toward a new method: the same quality, with compute costs cut by 90%.

This is not clickbait. Thinking Machines Lab reported measured results on the AIME 2024 math competition: the new method reached 74.4% accuracy with 1,800 GPU hours, while traditional reinforcement learning used 17,920 GPU hours and reached only 67.6%.

**Costs fell by tenfold, and performance improved.** In machine learning, this is close to a "free lunch."

The method is called On-Policy Distillation, abbreviated below as OPD. It comes from a Google DeepMind paper published at ICLR in early 2024: "On-Policy Distillation of Language Models: Learning from Self-Generated Mistakes."

Below, we break down three questions: what OPD actually does, why it works so well, and which companies are already using it.

---

## The Post-Training Dilemma for Large Language Models

To understand OPD, we first need to understand the problem it is trying to solve.

Large language model training has two stages: pretraining and post-training. Pretraining teaches the basic patterns of language from massive text corpora; post-training makes the model smarter and more aligned with user intent. The topic here is post-training.

Today, post-training mainly uses two methods, each with a serious weakness.

**Method 1: Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT)**

Prepare a batch of high-quality "question-answer" pairs and let the model learn by imitation. For example, show the model standard solutions to 100 math problems and ask it to copy the reasoning style.

The problem is that the model learns from the teacher's text, but at inference time it generates its own text. These two distributions are different. It is like reading 100 perfect-scoring essays, then writing in your own style: the mistakes you are likely to make may never appear in those perfect essays. The technical term is "train-test distribution mismatch," and it is the fundamental bottleneck of supervised fine-tuning.

**Method 2: Reinforcement Learning (RL)**

The model generates answers on its own, and then receives only final feedback on whether the result is correct.

The advantage is clear: the model learns from the text it generated itself, so there is no distribution mismatch. The disadvantage is equally clear. If a math solution has 50 steps and the only feedback is "wrong," the model has to guess which step caused the problem. This is called a "sparse reward." Learning from so little information requires enormous sampling and highly complex optimization algorithms, making training expensive and unstable.

![GKD outperforms traditional methods across all three tasks](../../assets/opd/fig1-gkd-performance.png)

Putting the two methods in a table makes the empty quadrant obvious:

| Method | Sampling method | Reward signal |
|------|--------|--------|
| Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) | Off-policy (text comes from teacher/expert) | Dense (token-by-token supervision) |
| Reinforcement Learning (RL) | On-policy (text generated by the student itself) | Sparse (only final outcome feedback) |
| **OPD (the focus here)** | **On-policy (text generated by the student itself)** | **Dense (teacher gives token-by-token feedback)** |

OPD's approach is simple: let the model learn from its own text, while also giving it precise feedback at every step. Does that sound like having both pieces of cake at once? Exactly. That is why it is called a "free lunch."

---

## Core Idea: The Student Solves the Problem, and the Teacher Marks It Step by Step

Return to a more intuitive analogy.

Suppose you are a new hire, and your company assigns you a senior mentor. Traditional training (SFT) means the mentor writes a model answer and you copy it once. Reinforcement learning means you solve the problem yourself, and at the end someone only tells you "pass" or "fail."

OPD does something very different: **you solve the problem in your own way, and then the mentor annotates your work line by line**: "this step is right," "this is where you started drifting," "this choice directly caused the later error."

The key difference is that the teacher comments on what the student actually wrote, not on a model answer the student would never produce.

In concrete training terms, the workflow has four steps:

**Step 1: The student generates text.** Give the student model a question and let it write a full answer using its current ability. These answers carry the student's own "accent" and "bad habits," which is exactly what we want.

**Step 2: The teacher scores each token.** Feed the student's answer into the teacher model, and let the teacher output its probability distribution for every token the student wrote. This computation is cheap: it only requires one forward pass and no sampling. This is one key reason OPD is cheaper than reinforcement learning. RL requires repeated sampling and evaluation; the teacher only needs to "read through" the student's answer once.

**Step 3: Compute the token-level gap.** At each token position, compare the teacher's probability distribution with the student's. This gap is a dense reward signal. It can tell the student precisely: at token 37, you made a poor choice, and the teacher would have gone in a very different direction.

**Step 4: Update the student model.** Use these token-level signals to update the student's parameters, so it makes better choices in similar positions next time.

---

## How Should the Teacher "Score" the Student?

This section goes a bit deeper into the technical details. If you want to skip the formulas, read the "plain-English translation" after each one and you will still understand the rest.

OPD uses KL divergence to measure the "gap between teacher and student." But KL divergence has two directions, and the choice matters enormously.

**Forward KL divergence** (used in traditional distillation):

$$D_{KL}(P_{teacher} \| P_{student}) = \sum_x P_{teacher}(x) \cdot \log \frac{P_{teacher}(x)}{P_{student}(x)}$$

Plain-English translation: you must study every topic the teacher might test. Every word the teacher considers possible must be covered by the student.

Effect: the student's output becomes more diffuse and conservative, touching a bit of everything but mastering nothing. The technical term is "mode covering."

**Reverse KL divergence** (used by OPD):

$$D_{KL}(P_{student} \| P_{teacher}) = \sum_x P_{student}(x) \cdot \log \frac{P_{student}(x)}{P_{teacher}(x)}$$

Plain-English translation: every topic you choose to study must be correct, but you do not need to cover every possible topic. Every direction selected by the student must also be accepted by the teacher.

Effect: the student concentrates on learning one strong strategy well, instead of spreading itself thin across all possibilities. The technical term is "mode seeking."

![Quality-diversity tradeoff between forward KL and reverse KL](../../assets/opd/fig2-kl-tradeoff.png)

Why is reverse KL better suited to large-model distillation? Because we do not need a small model to learn every capability of a large model; that is impossible. What we need is for the small model to do the things it can do as well as possible. Reverse KL encourages precisely that kind of "specialization" rather than "coverage."

There is also an elegant coincidence, which is really a mathematical necessity: reverse KL has to be computed on the student's own samples. Mathematically, it naturally requires that "the student solves the problem itself." Forward KL can be computed on teacher samples, so traditional distillation never needed the student to generate its own text.

The core design of "the student learns from its own work" is not only intuitive; it is also built into the loss function. Form and objective are perfectly aligned.

**From an information perspective:** In reinforcement learning, the model generates an answer with hundreds of tokens and receives only one final score. Each iteration provides only O(1) bits of information. In OPD, the teacher gives a full probability distribution at every token. If an answer has N tokens, each iteration provides O(N) bits of information. It is like grading an exam: reinforcement learning writes only a final score at the bottom of the paper, while OPD writes comments beside every question. The information content differs by two to three orders of magnitude.

That is the root reason OPD is so data-efficient.

