## Experimental Results: Let the Data Speak

The paper ran experiments on three very different tasks. Compared with traditional distillation, GKD delivered improvements of 1.7x to 2.1x. This was not a few percent better; it was more than twice as good in some settings.

| Task | Dataset | Improvement vs. traditional distillation |
|------|--------|----------------|
| Summarization | XSum | 2.1x |
| Machine translation | WMT | 1.7x |
| Math reasoning | GSM8K | 1.9x |

![GSM8K math reasoning comparison, where OPD outperforms all methods](../../assets/opd/fig4-gsm8k.png)

But the most striking finding concerns data efficiency:

![Data efficiency comparison: GKD with 5% of the data beats traditional distillation with 100% of the data](../../assets/opd/fig5-data-efficiency.png)

> **GKD using only 5% of the data outperformed traditional distillation using 100% of the data.**

If you have 100,000 training examples, running all of them with the traditional method performs worse than running only 5,000 examples with GKD.

The paper also includes a blockbuster result: a tiny model with only 77 million parameters, distilled through GKD, beat the 540 billion-parameter PaLM on multiple tasks. That is a 7,000x size gap, with the smaller model performing better.

![MMLU/BBH general capability comparison](../../assets/opd/fig6-mmlu-bbh.png)

It is not that small models cannot learn. The previous teaching method simply was not good enough.

