## Industrial Adoption: From Paper to Production

Start with a set of real-world results:

| Method | GPU hours | AIME'24 accuracy |
|------|---------|---------------|
| Traditional reinforcement learning | 17,920 | 67.6% |
| OPD | 1,800 | 74.4% |

These are Thinking Machines Lab's measured results on the American Invitational Mathematics Examination (October 2025). Cost fell by nearly tenfold, while accuracy rose by almost 7 percentage points.

Companies moved quickly:

**Google Gemma 2 (August 2024):** The paper authors' own organization used the method directly in Google's open-source model.

**Qwen3 (May 2025):** Alibaba's Tongyi Qianwen technical report was the first to systematically describe industrial-scale OPD practice. It clearly stated that OPD required only one-tenth of the GPU time of the full reinforcement learning pipeline while achieving comparable or better results.

**MiMo-V2-Flash (January 2026):** Validated OPD in multimodal settings, showing the method is not limited to pure text.

**GLM-5:** Zhipu also adopted similar on-policy distillation techniques in its next-generation model.

From a 2024 academic paper to a mainstream post-training paradigm in 2026, OPD has industrialized remarkably fast.

---

## OPD's Limits and Future

OPD has one structural prerequisite: **you must have a sufficiently strong teacher model.** In reinforcement learning, the reward signal can come from a rule-based verifier (for example, directly checking whether a math answer is correct), so it does not necessarily require a larger language model. In OPD, however, the teacher must be clearly stronger than the student on the target task.

This points to two especially important directions:

**Hybrid OPD + RL training.** Qwen3's practice already hints at this path: first use OPD to cheaply and stably improve baseline capability, then use RL for fine-grained tuning on specific dimensions. This may become the standard post-training workflow.

**Distilling reasoning models.** With the emergence of reasoning models such as o1/o3, efficiently distilling long-chain reasoning ability into smaller models is a highly promising use case for OPD. DeepSeek-R1 has already shown the enormous potential of distilling from large reasoning models into smaller ones.

In addition, self-distillation (letting the model serve as its own teacher) and multi-teacher fusion are also worth watching, though they remain in early exploration.

---

## Closing Thoughts

OPD's core insight is simple: students learn more effectively from their own mistakes than from copying someone else's correct answers. But "learning from your own mistakes" requires step-by-step guidance from a good teacher, not merely a final grade.

When we put that simple insight into a rigorous mathematical framework (reverse KL divergence + on-policy sampling), we get a method that cuts costs by an order of magnitude while improving results.

> In the second half of the large-model race, the winner may no longer be whoever spends the most money, but whoever trains more intelligently. OPD is the first shot in that shift, but it certainly will not be the last.

